WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.120
Growth sprout.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.680
Biting my time.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.080
Duck tape.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.520
Two peas in a pond.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.080
Escape goat.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.960
You know that mortifying moment

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.800
when you use a common phrase
in front of everyone,

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.440
only to find out you've been
saying it wrong for years?

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.400
Well, the good news is you can
stop that shame spiral in its tracks

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.480
because, according to the nice folks
at the Oxford English Dictionary,

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.680
we've all done it.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.840
Mishearing popular idioms,
metaphors and sayings

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.440
is much more common
than you'd think and actually

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.760
if enough people
make the same mistake

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.400
it can come to take root
in our collective consciousness.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.320
Sometimes even replacing
the original phrasing entirely.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.040
For example,
if someone were wildly mistaken,

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.440
would you say they had
another thing coming

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.160
or another think coming?

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:47.680
Believe it or not,

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.520
according to Oxford's
language monitoring databases

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.480
the original phrase was
another think coming,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.760
which to lots of you
probably sounds all kinds of wrong.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.520
If someone strips down
to their birthday suit,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.680
would you say they were
butt naked or buck naked?

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.520
While buck naked
is the earlier form,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.080
nobody would bat an eyelid

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.320
or, to misuse a common phrase,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.600
an eyelash, if you were
to say 'butt naked'.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.080
These are the sort of changes

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.080
that keep lexicographers busy
updating their dictionaries

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.440
so that they reflect how language
is really being used by people,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.080
rather than instruct on how
language should be used.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.480
So tell that to the next pedant
who tries to correct you.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.920
In fact, many common turns of
phrase have had fascinating journeys

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.280
evolving into the popular sayings
we all know and love today.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.080
To curry favour, meaning to
ingratiate yourself with someone,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.320
has nothing to do with buttering them
up by buying them a vindaloo.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.440
Its original form was actually
to curry Favel,

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.600
which will make
absolutely no sense to you

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.520
unless you've brushed up
on your medieval French literature.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.120
Favel, or Fauvel,

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.600
was the name of a horse
in an early 14th Century poem

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.240
who was renowned
for his cunning and duplicity.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.960
To curry Favel meant to groom
him with a special comb,

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.760
still called a curry comb today.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.560
Favel was commonly misheard as favour

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.240
and the rest, as they say,
is history.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.720
Basically the term 'to curry favour'
has lived such a rich life

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.680
it would probably have
a much better Tinder bio than you.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.520
Social media in general
has proved the perfect place

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.640
to mercilessly take the mickey out of
some of the more amusing mishearings.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.520
Linguist Geoffrey Pullum
coined the term eggcorn

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.240
to describe these
idiosyncratic substitutions

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.640
after a woman famously said "eggcorn"
when she meant "acorn".

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:37.960
This name stuck,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.800
and has since spawned many a listicle
of people's favourite examples,

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.120
from the fairly logical damp squid

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.240
for damp squib - squib being now a
little-known word for a firework -

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.920
to the cringeworthy

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.560
all intensive purposes for
all intents and purposes.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.920
While eggcorns can be great fun,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.800
nothing brings people together
like a misheard line or lyric.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.760
Since the 1950s

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.880
these have been known as mondegreens

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.880
after American writer Sylvia Wright

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.120
described mishearing
a line from a poem

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.600
"Laid him on the green"

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.760
as "Lady Mondegreen".

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.400
Even Taylor Swift isn't immune
to a mondegreen,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.240
with listeners widely hearing,
"Long list of ex-lovers"

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.000
in Blank Space as,

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.960
"Lonely Starbucks lovers".

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.880
Another lol-worthy
linguistic muddle up

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.200
is the malaphor -

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.840
a blend of malapropism

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.280
the mistaken use of a word
in place of a similar-sounding one,

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:32.400
and metaphor.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.840
A malaphor is a mixed up idiom.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.000
For example,
"It's not rocket surgery"

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.000
or "It'll be a walk in the cake."

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.000
So next time you're at a party
and you say

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.240
"We'll burn that bridge
when we get to it,"

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.560
"The cows came home to roost,"

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.440
or "Every cloud has a silver spoon
in its mouth," remember,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.800
you're not making a mistake

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.480
you're contributing to the evolution
of the English language.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.400
You're basically Shakespeare,
and one of these days your mistake

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.720
might just end up
in the English dictionary.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.800
What's your most embarrassing
malaphor or eggcorn?

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:11.880
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

